From a6121cf354f70a66c5f482f5e7a7992f6ee5b2b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W1nkelm4nn <mrmarkap61@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 00:07:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: Build Metal pipelines on a compile queue

vkCreate*Pipelines waited for Metal to build each pipeline, so a game
compiled at most as many pipelines at once as it had threads creating
them. Horizon Zero Dawn creates its pipelines on six threads while
Metal's compiler processes could use all cores.

The MSL is still generated during pipeline creation, but the Metal
libraries and pipeline states of compute shaders and of the VS that
holds a graphics pipeline are now built on a device queue with one
thread per core. Binding a shader waits for its build, or builds it
on the calling thread if the queue has not started it yet. Destroying
a shader drops its build if it has not started. Draws and dispatches
with a pipeline Metal could not build are skipped instead of failing
pipeline creation.

Horizon Zero Dawn's first start with empty caches, 15 minutes: Metal
built 8,644 instead of 4,240 pipelines, its compiler used 6.4 instead
of 3.3 cores. Rise of the Tomb Raider's benchmark with warm caches is
unchanged at 27.1 fps.

